WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.470
I don’t ever want to talk about being a woman scientist again.
我再也不想谈论身为女科学家这件事了。

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:12.350
There was a time in my life when people asked constantly for stories about what it’s like to work in a field dominated by men.
我人生中曾有一段时间，人们总是请我讲些故事，讲讲在男性主导的领域工作是什么感受。

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:18.300
I was never very good at telling those stories because truthfully I never found them interesting.
我从来都不太会讲这些故事，因为说实话，我从来不觉得它们有意思。

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:25.450
What I do find interesting is the origin of the universe, the shape of space-time and the nature of black holes.
我真正感兴趣的，是宇宙的起源、时空的形态和黑洞的本质。

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:33.280
At 19, when I began studying astrophysics, it did not bother me in the least to be the only woman in the classroom.
19岁开始学天体物理时，我是教室里唯一的女生，但我对此一点也不在意。

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:41.680
But while I was earning my Ph.D. at MIT and then working as a postdoc doing space research, the issue started to bother me.
但在麻省理工学院读博士期间，以及后来做太空研究的博士后时，这个问题开始困扰我。

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:48.940
My every achievement—jobs, research papers, awards—was viewed through the lens of gender politics.
我的每一项成就——工作、研究论文、奖项——都被放在性别政治的视角下审视。

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:50.930
So were my failures.
我的失败也是如此。

00:00:51.730 --> 00:01:02.910
Sometimes, when I was pushed into an argument on left brain versus right brain, or nature versus nurture, I would instantly fight fiercely on my own behalf and on behalf of all womankind.
有时，当我被卷入关于左脑与右脑、先天与后天的争论时，我会立刻为自己、也为全体女性激烈地争辩。

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:13.192
Then one day a few years ago, out of my mouth came a sentence that would eventually become my reply to any and all provocations: I don’t talk about that anymore.
后来，几年前的一天，我脱口说出了一句话，这句话最终成了我对一切挑衅的回答：我不再谈论那个话题了。

00:01:13.992 --> 00:01:20.832
It took me 10 years to get back the confidence I had at 19 and to realize that I didn’t want to deal with gender issues.
我花了10年时间，才找回19岁时的那份自信，并意识到自己不想去应付性别问题。

00:01:21.632 --> 00:01:26.692
Why should curing sexism be yet another terrible burden on every female scientist?
为什么消除性别歧视要成为每一位女科学家的又一个沉重负担？

00:01:27.492 --> 00:01:31.122
After all, I don’t study sociology or political theory.
毕竟，我研究的又不是社会学或政治理论。

00:01:31.922 --> 00:01:36.082
Today I research and teach at Barnard, a women’s college in New York City.
如今，我在巴纳德学院——纽约市的一所女子学院——做研究并授课。

00:01:36.882 --> 00:01:41.472
Recently, someone asked me how many of the 45 students in my class were women.
最近，有人问我班上45名学生中有多少是女生。

00:01:42.272 --> 00:01:47.022
You cannot imagine my satisfaction at being able to answer, 45.
你想象不到，我能回答“45名”时有多满足。

00:01:47.822 --> 00:01:53.732
I know some of my students worry about how they will manage their scientific research and a desire for children.
我知道，我的一些学生担心将来如何兼顾科研和想要孩子的愿望。

00:01:54.532 --> 00:01:56.362
And I don’t dismiss those concerns.
我并不轻视这些顾虑。

00:01:57.162 --> 00:01:59.122
Still, I don’t tell them “war” stories.
不过，我不会给她们讲那些“战斗”故事。

00:01:59.922 --> 00:02:06.564
Instead, I have given them this: the visual of their physics professor heavily pregnant doing physics experiments.
相反，我给了她们这样一个画面：她们的物理教授挺着大肚子做物理实验。

00:02:07.364 --> 00:02:12.284
And in turn they have given me the image of 45 women driven by a love of science.
反过来，她们也给了我这样一幅图景：45名女性因热爱科学而奋进。

00:02:13.084 --> 00:02:14.914
And that’s a sight worth talking about.
这才是值得一谈的景象。
